course: dictionary
lesson: 14788
title: Publicise
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To publicise something means to make it known to many people. You often do this by advertising it, or by telling people about it in the newspaper, on the radio or on posters. «The school publicised the bake sale with bright posters in every corridor.» The posters let lots of people know about the sale.
explain: Look at the clues around the word. «The club wants to publicise its new football team» tells you the club wants many people to know about the team. Ask yourself: what word would fit here? Telling people about would fit, so publicise means to make known.
explain: Publicise is a verb, so its ending can change: publicises, publicised, publicising. «Yesterday the newspaper publicised the summer fair.» To check your meaning, swap in make known to many people. If the sentence still makes sense, you have it right.
fact: Publicise means to make information about something known to many people.
fact: You can publicise something by advertising it, or by reporting it in the news.
fact: In British English it is spelled publicise; in American English it is spelled publicize.
fact: The phrase «widely publicised» means known by a great many people.
fact: Publicising a bake sale means telling people about it, so more people come to buy cakes.
exercise: «The pupils made posters to publicise the bake sale.» What does publicise mean here? | *To make the bake sale known to many people | To buy all the cakes before the sale | Hide the bake sale from everyone | To clean the hall after the sale
exercise: «The newspaper publicised the new library.» Which word means nearly the same as publicised? | forgot | ignored | hid | *announced
exercise: «The council will publicise the road works in the local paper.» Find and copy the word that means to make known. | council | paper | *publicise | road
test: «Mia wants to publicise her charity walk, so she makes flyers and tells the local radio.» Why does Mia do this? | So that she can stop the walk | So the walk will be cancelled | So that she can hide from her friends | *So that many people will know about the walk
test: Which sentence uses publicise correctly? | The cleaners publicised the floor by sweeping it. | *The shop publicised its sale with an advert in the paper. | The teacher publicised the bell at the end of break. | The dog publicised its bone under the table.
exam: «The club will publicise its summer camp.» What is the closest meaning of publicise? | *To tell many people about the camp | To pack up the camp | To hide the camp from families | To cancel the camp
